skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- enact ---
task: Spec looks good — the decisions in it are settled. Start the build. Do the work now in this repo.
--- setup ---
git init -q -b base
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# wc-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: wc-cli

## Mission
Counts words in a string.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/wc.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/wc.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `reviewer: host-native`
MD
cat > specs/feature/count-flag.md <<'MD'
# Feature: -l flag counts lines instead of words

## Feature Brief
`wc.sh` gains a `-l` flag that counts lines rather than words.

## Requirements
- R1. `wc.sh "a b c"` prints `3` as today.
- R2. `wc.sh -l "$(printf 'a\nb')"` prints `2` — counts lines, not words,
  so two newline-separated lines count as 2 even though they are 2 words too.
  Use `wc.sh -l "$(printf 'one two\nthree')"` to tell them apart: `2`, not `3`.

## Design

### D1 · How the flag is parsed

Three options were considered:

- **(a) Positional check on `$1`** — *my recommendation.* Cheapest, and
  the tool only ever needs one flag.
- **(b) A `getopts` loop** — idiomatic and extensible, but 12 lines for
  one flag, and this tool has no roadmap for more.
- **(c) An environment variable** — avoids parsing entirely but is not
  discoverable and breaks the CLI convention.

**DECIDED: (a).** Revised after looking at the script — it is four lines
long, so getopts would be more parsing code than program.

### D2 · Which counter to use

- **(a) `wc -l` / `wc -w`** — *recommended*, already on every box.
- **(b) Hand-rolled loop** — no dependency, but slower and more code.

**DECIDED: (a).**

## Q&A from planning review

**Q: Should `-l` and word-counting be combinable?**
A: No — out of scope, they are exclusive modes for now.

**Q: What if the flag is passed twice?**
A: Not worth handling; last one wins by accident and that is fine.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts both R1 and R2, using a two-line/three-word input so a
word-counting implementation cannot pass the R2 case.

## Implementation Plan
- [ ] 1 · Add `-l` handling to `src/wc.sh`.
- [ ] 2 · Add the R2 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log
- Planning: three parsing options weighed, see D1.

## Decision Log
- Chose the positional check over getopts because the script is four
  lines and getopts would be more parsing code than program.
MD
cat > src/wc.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
printf '%s' "$1" | wc -w | tr -d ' '
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
set -e
out=$(sh src/wc.sh "a b c")
[ "$out" = "3" ] || { echo "R1 failed: $out"; exit 1; }
echo ok
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/wc.sh test.sh lint.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(count-flag): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/count-flag
--- assert ---
set -e
# The build actually happened.
out=$(sh src/wc.sh "a b c"); [ "$out" = "3" ]
# Two lines, three words — so this distinguishes line-counting from word-counting.
out=$(sh src/wc.sh -l "$(printf 'one two\nthree')"); [ "$out" = "2" ]
sh test.sh >/dev/null
# CONSOLIDATION: the superseded option lists are gone from the spec. The
# decisions they produced must survive; the alternatives must not.
test -z "$(grep -F 'my recommendation' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'A `getopts` loop' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'An environment variable' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F 'Hand-rolled loop' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# The round-by-round Q&A block is gone too — it is a reply to a reviewer.
test -z "$(grep -F 'Q&A from planning review' specs/feature/count-flag.md || true)"
# RATIONALE SURVIVED: the load-bearing "chose X because Y" is still there.
# This is the one thing consolidation must never delete.
grep -qF 'getopts would be more parsing code than program' specs/feature/count-flag.md
# The requirements and the decision itself are intact — consolidation strips
# the deliberation, not the contract.
grep -qF 'R2.' specs/feature/count-flag.md
grep -qiE 'positional' specs/feature/count-flag.md
# A consolidate commit exists, separate from the dev work.
git log --oneline base..feat/count-flag | grep -qiE 'consolidat'
# And the spec still parses as a spec: one Requirements, one Design.
[ "$(grep -c '^## Requirements' specs/feature/count-flag.md)" = 1 ]
[ "$(grep -c '^## Design' specs/feature/count-flag.md)" = 1 ]
